"""Topics and explanations for CPRITE 2023 Q151–200."""

META: dict[int, tuple[str, str]] = {
    151: ("Psychotherapy",
         "Biofeedback trains voluntary control of autonomic functions (heart rate, skin temperature, muscle tension) by feeding those signals back in real time and reinforcing the desired change -- classic operant conditioning. Hypnosis relies on suggestion and focused attention, not operant shaping of ANS output. Autogenic therapy uses self-relaxation phrases. TMS is electromagnetic cortical stimulation. EMDR pairs bilateral stimulation with trauma recall. Teaching point: Biofeedback = operant control of the autonomic nervous system."),
    152: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "Omega-3 PUFAs (EPA/DHA) are keyed as suppressing proinflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, TNF-alpha), a proposed antidepressant mechanism in inflammatory depression. They are not NMDA antagonists, SSRIs, or agents that promote microglial proliferation (they tend to shift microglia toward a less inflammatory phenotype). Gut-flora change is speculative and not the board answer. Teaching point: Fish oil's mood story = turn down inflammatory cytokines."),
    153: ("Psychopathology",
         "Poor eye contact, weak social initiation, missed social cues, distress at unexpected transitions, rule-bound rigidity, preference for aloneness, and a circumscribed solar-system interest is autism spectrum disorder. Social anxiety wants friends but fears scrutiny and lacks restricted interests/rigidity. OCD is unwanted rituals, not a social-communication phenotype. ADHD is inattention/hyperactivity without this social-communication plus RRB package. Social (pragmatic) communication disorder is the social-language piece without restricted/repetitive behavior. Teaching point: Social-communication deficits plus rigidity/special interests = ASD, not social anxiety or SCD."),
    154: ("Neuroscience",
         "Seizures, language regression, and hyperactivity point to Landau-Kleffner / epileptic encephalopathy with continuous spike-and-wave in sleep (CSWS/ESES). Slow spike-and-wave is Lennox-Gastaut. Occipital spikes that fade with eye opening are childhood occipital epilepsy. Brief 4 Hz generalized bursts suggest juvenile myoclonic (or atypical absence) epilepsy, not this language-regression picture. Polymorphic spikes are nonspecific. Teaching point: Language loss + seizures + hyperactivity --> EEG for continuous spike-and-wave in sleep."),
    155: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "For gender dysphoria at Tanner 2, the first medical step after psychotherapy and social support is a GnRH agonist to pause endogenous puberty. Cross-sex hormones come later (typically mid-adolescence after further assessment). Spironolactone is an antiandrogen used in later feminizing regimens, not the Tanner-2 start. Surgery is last, not first. Oxytocin is not a gender-affirming intervention. Teaching point: Tanner 2 GD medical start = puberty blockade with a GnRH agonist."),
    156: ("Psychotherapy",
         "'Nobody wants to be my friend anymore' after one argument is overgeneralization: a single event is stretched into a global, always/everyone rule. Absolutism/all-or-nothing is black-versus-white with no middle. Personalization is 'this is my fault.' Catastrophizing jumps to the worst possible outcome. Attending to negatives is a mental filter that ignores positives without necessarily generalizing from one incident. Teaching point: One fight --> 'nobody ever' = overgeneralization."),
    157: ("Psychotherapy",
         "Family therapy for ODD is keyed as restoring a warm, mutually respectful parent-child relationship -- the vehicle that lets limits stick (PCIT/FFT-style warmth plus authority). The therapist does not become the child's limit-setter. Abuse monitoring is a safety duty, not the treatment goal. Psychoeducation alone does not change coercive cycles. Mapping whether family boundaries are diffuse or rigid is structural assessment, not the primary goal. Teaching point: ODD family work succeeds when warmth and respect between parent and child are rebuilt."),
    158: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "New motor restlessness and inability to stay seated after an antipsychotic is akathisia; first-line is a beta-blocker (propranolol). Benztropine treats dystonia/parkinsonism and is a weak akathisia drug. Buspirone is not first-line. Carbamazepine is irrelevant. Methylphenidate can worsen restlessness. Teaching point: Antipsychotic akathisia --> propranolol, not benztropine."),
    159: ("Development",
         "A 30-month-old stacking blocks beside a peer, with the adult exchanging toys, is parallel play -- expected at 2-3 years -- and showing the caregiver the blocks is normal social referencing, not a failed share. Associative/cooperative play comes later. This is not ADHD, separation anxiety, attachment failure, or ASD. Teaching point: Side-by-side play at 2.5 years is parallel play, and it is normal."),
    160: ("Consultation & Schools",
         "The One-Minute Preceptor starts with get a commitment: 'What is your DSM diagnosis?' Probe for evidence is next ('most important rule-out'). Praising then naming the miss, asking whether mania was covered, and 'how do you think that went' skip the commitment step and jump to feedback or self-assessment. Teaching point: OMP first move = make the learner commit to a diagnosis."),
    161: ("Psychopathology",
         "Most youth with conduct disorder improve substantially by young adulthood; a minority persist into adult antisocial personality. 'Likely incarcerated' overstates continuity. Medication does not reliably produce remission. Progressive decline before improvement is not the typical course. Data are ample enough to counsel this prognosis. Teaching point: Most CD does not become lifetime criminality -- most kids get better."),
    162: ("Development",
         "Ages 5-12 are Erikson's industry versus inferiority: a sense of competence (school, peers, skills) is the main engine of emotional development. Object permanence is infancy. Language acquisition is toddlerhood. Formal operations and self-actualization are adolescent/later. Teaching point: Middle childhood emotional growth rides on feeling able to do things well."),
    163: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "In a forensic child evaluation the client is the court or retaining agency, not the child; there is no treating physician-patient relationship, no fiduciary duty to relieve suffering, and confidentiality is limited to the legal process. Therapeutic bias is a risk to be minimized, not increased. Teaching point: Forensic eval -- the court is the client, not the child."),
    164: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "HIPAA's Privacy Rule carves out psychotherapy notes (the therapist's process notes, kept separate from the medical record) as a special category that generally needs patient authorization to release, beyond ordinary TPO. HIV, SUD, gender-dysphoria, and inpatient records have other protections but are not this HIPAA 'psychotherapy notes' bucket. Teaching point: HIPAA extra lockbox = psychotherapy notes."),
    165: ("Consultation & Schools",
         "Statewide Child Psychiatry Access Programs (MCPAP-style) let the pediatrician reach a child psychiatrist by telephone or electronic consult -- not a psychiatrist sitting in the school, not an embedded clinic psychiatrist (collaborative-care colocation), not resident rotations, and not weekly virtual rounds on a shared panel. Teaching point: CPAP = pediatrician phones/e-consults a child psychiatrist."),
    166: ("Psychotherapy",
         "'It is wonderful that you have been sober for six months' is an affirmation: the clinician names a patient strength or effort. Empathy is understanding feeling. Reflection restates content/meaning. Reframing offers a new interpretation. Summarizing gathers several strands. Teaching point: Praise of a specific patient effort = MI affirmation."),
    167: ("Consultation & Schools",
         "A school consultant after a student suicide advises administrators on response (communication, memorials, contagion risk). Prescribing, running student groups, and evaluating staff or students for depression are clinical/treating roles, not consultation. Teaching point: Consultant = counsel the system; do not become the school's therapist."),
    168: ("Neuroscience",
         "Imprinting silences the maternal or paternal copy of a gene (parent-of-origin epigenetic shutdown), as in Prader-Willi/Angelman at 15q. It is not later repression after activity, intron splicing, polyadenylation, or increasing expression across generations (that is anticipation). Teaching point: Imprinting = one parent's allele is silenced."),
    169: ("Psychopathology",
         "Schizotypal personality already has few friends, odd fantasies/superpowers, vague speech, and fearful affect. Social anxiety that does not ease with familiarity supports the diagnosis and helps separate it from social anxiety disorder (which usually lessens with known people). Stereotypies suggest ASD. Mood lability/outbursts suggest DMDD/BPD. Separation fear is SAD. Frank persecutory/religious delusions push toward a psychotic disorder. Teaching point: Schizotypal social anxiety stays up even with familiar people."),
    170: ("Assessment",
         "The classic ADHD soft sign is difficulty with rapid repetitive/alternating motor tasks (dysdiadochokinesia, overflow). Vertical saccades, abnormal DTRs, a snout reflex, and Gowers-type difficulty rising from prone are not the ADHD soft-sign cluster. Teaching point: ADHD neuro exam extra = clumsy rapid alternating movements."),
    171: ("Research Methods",
         "For a rare condition, the boards want case-control: sample already-diagnosed children and compare them with controls, which is efficient when the disorder is uncommon. A prospective cohort or RCT needs a huge at-risk pool. Cross-sectional is a snapshot, not 'over time.' Meta-analysis needs existing trials. (A longitudinal cohort of identified cases is conceptually close to 'outcomes over time,' but the keyed design for rare disease is case-control.) Teaching point: Rare disease --> case-control."),
    172: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "Cohen 2015 JAACAP meta-analysis of placebo-controlled stimulant trials found no association between new-onset or worsening tics and psychostimulant use (risk similar to placebo; type, dose, and duration did not change it). Long- or short-acting methylphenidate or dextroamphetamine were not shown to increase tics. Teaching point: Controlled data do not show stimulants causing tics -- coincidence is more likely than causation."),
    173: ("Neuroscience",
         "Anticipation is worsening (and often earlier onset) across generations because a triplet repeat expands (Huntington, myotonic dystrophy, Fragile X). Imprinting is parent-of-origin silencing. Duplication is extra copy number. Mosaicism is two cell lines in one person. Polymorphism is a common variant. Teaching point: Expanding triplet repeats across generations = anticipation."),
    174: ("Development",
         "The unexpected-transfer false-belief task: a typical 3-year-old says the child will look on the shelf (where the doll actually is) because theory of mind -- representing another's false belief -- is not yet reliable (it consolidates around 4). Object permanence is infancy. Social referencing is looking to a caregiver for how to feel. Conservation is concrete operations. Transitional phenomena are Winnicott (blanket/teddy). Teaching point: Age 3 fails Sally-Anne because theory of mind is still coming online."),
    175: ("Psychopathology",
         "Gory images of family being harmed that 'pop in' many times a day, with anxious checking that people are safe, are intrusive harm obsessions -- OCD -- not a psychotic 'vision.' The remote minor car accident is not a qualifying PTSD trauma with re-experiencing of that event. SAD would center on separation; GAD is free-floating worry, not discrete ego-dystonic images. Schizophreniform needs a broader psychosis picture. Teaching point: Recurrent unwanted harm pictures plus checking = OCD, not PTSD or psychosis."),
    176: ("Psychopathology",
         "Among the listed factors, chronic NSSI is the strongest predictor of later suicide attempts (and a robust marker of suicide risk). Depression, anxiety, impulsivity, and BPD all raise risk but, head-to-head in this item, lose to repeated self-injury. Teaching point: Chronic cutting is a suicide-attempt predictor, not 'just a coping skill.'"),
    177: ("Systems & Prevention",
         "The Child Guidance Clinic movement (Healy's Juvenile Psychopathic Institute, 1909, and the later Commonwealth Fund clinics) began as a response to juvenile delinquency, marrying psychiatry, psychology, and social work around court-involved youth. It was not founded to treat poverty, child abuse, special education, or deinstitutionalization. Teaching point: Child guidance started with delinquent kids, not with abuse or poverty."),
    178: ("Consultation & Schools",
         "IDEA guarantees FAPE and education in the least restrictive environment. It does not entitle the child to PMT, an on-site psychiatrist, a private tutor, or subsidized individual psychotherapy. Teaching point: IDEA = FAPE in the least restrictive environment."),
    179: ("Psychopathology",
         "Encopresis clusters with externalizing disorders; oppositional defiant disorder is the most common comorbid psychiatric diagnosis among these choices (ADHD is also common but not listed). Anxiety and depression occur less often as the lead comorbidity. Teaching point: Soiling --> look for ODD (and ADHD), not GAD first."),
    180: ("Psychopathology",
         "NLSY97 trajectory work (Kelly and Vuolo) found early-heavy-quitters and persistent heavy users had similar adult-role outcomes -- heavy adolescent use was the hit, even if they quit. Dabblers resembled abstainers on school/work. Infrequent users were not the most sexually conservative. Early quitters were not uniformly less likely to have children (parenthood was the exception). Abstainers did not outclass every other group on education. Teaching point: Heavy teen marijuana use leaves a similar adult scar whether or not they quit early."),
    181: ("Neuroscience",
         "BDNF supports synaptogenesis and reduces apoptosis in the toddler brain (activity-dependent trophic support). Leptin and agouti-related peptide are energy-balance peptides. Hypocretin/orexin stabilizes wake. Anandamide is an endocannabinoid. Teaching point: Toddler synapse-building peptide they want = BDNF."),
    182: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "Hyperthermia, tachycardia, dehydration, and rhabdomyolysis in a substance-using teen, with NMS also on the list, is the MDMA (ecstasy) toxidrome -- serotonin excess, heat, and muscle breakdown. Alcohol, cannabis, heroin, and GHB do not produce this hot, tachycardic, rhabdo picture as the leading toxidrome. Teaching point: Hot, tachycardic rhabdo + party drug --> MDMA (and still rule out NMS)."),
    183: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "Malpractice's 4 Ds are duty, dereliction, direct cause, and damages. A forensic psychiatrist examining the abused child in a suit against the school is there to characterize harm -- damages -- not to decide the school's duty, whether staff were derelict, or legal liability (those are for the court). Teaching point: Child forensic exam in a lawsuit --> assess damages, not liability."),
    184: ("Research Methods",
         "NNT of 9 means you treat nine patients for one extra responder attributable to drug versus placebo. Many of the nine may improve (placebo plus drug), but only one of those improvements is the drug's incremental effect. It is not 'nine times more likely' (that is a ratio), not a 1-in-9 total response rate, and not the placebo response. Teaching point: NNT 9 = one extra drug-attributable response per nine treated."),
    185: ("Research Methods",
         "Sensitivity = TP / (TP + FN) = 85 / (85 + 15) = 85%. Specificity = 99 / (99 + 1) = 99%. NPV is about 87%; PPV is about 99%. Teaching point: Sensitivity = true positives / all who actually have the disease."),
    186: ("Development",
         "Recent US/meta-analytic data put girls' pubertal onset (thelarche, Tanner 2 breast) around 8-10 years, earlier than the old Marshall-Tanner 11-13 figure. 5-7 is premature in most girls. 14-16 is delayed. Menarche remains ~12-13; this item is about starting puberty, not menses. Teaching point: US girls now typically start puberty at 8-10, not 11-13."),
    187: ("Systems & Prevention",
         "For refugee youth, close friendships are the psychosocial factor that most consistently protects mental health (belonging, proxy family, daily support). Elite schools, pressure to assimilate fast, and frequent news from the country of origin are not the keyed protective factor and can even add stress. Teaching point: Refugee-youth resilience --> several close friends, not a ranked school or forced assimilation."),
    188: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "After verbal de-escalation fails, next is an extra oral dose of the child's usual aripiprazole, reduce environmental stimulation, and treat GI pain/constipation -- a common driver of ASD aggression. IM lorazepam or olanzapine is more restrictive and not first if the child can take PO. Hydroxyzine is a weak answer. Sending caregivers out often escalates autistic kids. Inpatient transfer is disposition, not de-escalation. Teaching point: ASD ED agitation --> PO home antipsychotic, quieter room, check the belly."),
    189: ("Psychopathology",
         "With affective instability, impulsivity, and recurrent self-harm already present, DSM borderline is supported by chronic emptiness, frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, and transient dissociation (or stress-related paranoia). Entitlement and exploiting others are narcissistic/antisocial. Persistent paranoia is more psychotic/paranoid PD. Inappropriate seductiveness is histrionic. Flashbacks are PTSD. Teaching point: Add emptiness + abandonment panic + brief dissociation to lock in adolescent BPD."),
    190: ("Psychopharmacology",
         "In liver failure, the lower-hepatotoxicity antidepressants on this list are citalopram, escitalopram, and fluvoxamine. Duloxetine, nefazodone, amitriptyline, bupropion, and trazodone carry higher DILI signals (nefazodone enough to withdraw it in some markets). Teaching point: Liver-sick depression --> citalopram, escitalopram, fluvoxamine; skip duloxetine and nefazodone."),
    191: ("Psychotherapy",
         "Evidence-based pediatric OCD CBT (March/Mulle, POTS) uses graded exposure (ERP hierarchy), renaming/externalizing the thought ('that's OCD'), and active assertion ('bossing back' OCD). Attachment work, self-concept, psychodrama, and detriangulation are not the OCD package. Accepting thoughts/feelings is more ACT than the keyed child protocol. Teaching point: Kid OCD therapy = expose in steps, name the OCD, and talk back to it."),
    192: ("Development",
         "Between 18 and 36 months (Piaget stage 6 into early preoperational), symbolic/pretend play, deferred imitation, and internal (insight) problem-solving come online and change how the child relates. Object permanence and cause-and-effect are earlier (infancy). Trial-and-error problem-solving is 12-18 months (tertiary circular reactions). Conservation is ~7 years. Identity consolidation is adolescence. Teaching point: 18-36 months = pretend, copy-later, and think-then-act."),
    193: ("Neuroscience",
         "Long-term after moderate-severe pediatric TBI: word-finding difficulty, loss of previously acquired skills, and a slower rate of new learning (kids 'grow into' deficits as school demands rise). Full aphasic syndromes are more adult. Retrograde amnesia often fades. Visuospatial construction can be hit but is not keyed. Intrusive trauma thoughts are PTSD, not a neurocognitive sequela. Parietal seizures are not the typical chronic package. Teaching point: Kid TBI late effects = anomia, skill loss, and slowed learning -- not adult-style aphasia."),
    194: ("Consultation & Schools",
         "To trigger an SLD/IEP evaluation the letter should document that vision (and hearing) problems are ruled out, that the child failed high-quality reading interventions (RTI), and that emotional factors are not the main cause. Cultural disadvantage, ELL status, a poor school, low IQ (that is ID), and motor impairment are exclusionary or different eligibility paths, not the three SLD starters. Teaching point: IEP-for-reading letter = not eyes, not feelings, and already failed good reading help."),
    195: ("Psychopathology",
         "DSM-5 will not let you diagnose DMDD together with bipolar disorder, intermittent explosive disorder, or ODD (ODD is considered subsumed). DMDD can coexist with ADHD, MDD, conduct disorder, PTSD, and substance use. Teaching point: DMDD excludes bipolar, IED, and ODD."),
    196: ("Psychopathology",
         "True on this item: social media use has a dose-dependent link to later depression; negative online experiences worsen depressive symptoms; problematic use tracks with social anxiety and rejection sensitivity. MDD does not reliably precede problematic use as the main arrow. Positive experiences do not cleanly improve depression. Depression-as-confounder-for-sleep is not the keyed claim. More use is not protective in marginalized youth here, and more 'likes' are not tied to lower anxiety/higher self-esteem. Teaching point: More time + worse interactions + rejection-sensitive problematic use --> worse mood."),
    197: ("Neuroscience",
         "Default mode network hubs keyed here are medial prefrontal cortex, posterior cingulate, and medial posterior parietal cortex (precuneus). Insula and ACC are salience. DLPFC is central executive. Lateral posterior parietal is more attention/executive. Ventromedial PFC is a DMN neighbor but not one of the three letters they want (they chose medial PFC, not the vmPFC option). Teaching point: DMN = mPFC + PCC + medial parietal (precuneus)."),
    198: ("Assessment",
         "A preschooler who bolts upright, screams, looks terrified, sweats, and is tachycardic: highest on the list are sleep terrors (NREM, first-third-of-night, autonomic, amnestic), nocturnal epilepsy, and panic attacks. Somnambulism is walking, not this freeze-and-scream picture. Narcolepsy is daytime. RLS is sensory/urge. OSA is snore/apnea. Circadian disorders shift clock, they do not produce this paroxysm. Teaching point: Night sit-up scream + autonomic storm --> terrors, seizure, panic."),
    199: ("Assessment",
         "Anorexia commonly elevates cortisol (starvation stress), cholesterol (odd but classic), and liver enzymes (starvation hepatitis). Sodium, chloride, potassium, thyroxine, and WBC are typically low or low-normal, not high. Teaching point: Restricting labs that can be high = cortisol, cholesterol, LFTs."),
    200: ("Ethics & Forensics",
         "Joint Commission: continuous staff monitoring while restrained, release as soon as the child is calm, and a debrief with the patient afterward. There is no required 15-minute minimum. Do not chain seclusion or 24-hour isolation/constant observation as automatic next steps, and do not require a post-restraint antihistamine. Teaching point: Restraints -- watch continuously, stop when calm, then debrief."),
}
